  "updated": "2026-08-10T02:36:47.582074+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure is in control, and with NW flow across the\narea, much of the humidity has dissipated. Dewpoints in the\nmid to upper 50s across the area with highs in the upper 70s to\nlow 80s will make for a wonderful afternoon and evening. Mostly\nclear skies tonight will allow lows to fall into the upper 50s\nto low 60s.\n\nA Ring of Fire weather pattern looks to set up for much of the\nweek next week, starting on Monday. The Bermuda high will slide\nto the SW tonight into Monday and merge with the ridge that has \nset up shop over over the western US for the past few weeks.\nThis will open a corridor of active weather from the western\nGreat Lakes SE into the Mid Atlantic for much of the week. The\nridge will provide the hot, humid air needed for convection\nwhile shortwaves riding the NE edge of the ridge will provide\nthe shear for storm organization. Several Mesoscale Convective\nSystems are expected to develop over the northern Plains and\nfollow the ridge into the Mid-Atlantic region. The first one is\nexpected Monday and is currently the only one expected to impact\nour region through mid-week. This system should crash through\nthe Great Lakes, entering western New York by the mid-morning\nhours. A morning arrival and clouds ahead of this system will \nlimit the instability available for strong to severe\nthunderstorms to develop. By late morning to early afternoon, \nMLCAPE values increase to 1000-1500 j/kg and 0-6km shear\nincreases from the teens to 30-35kts as the afternoon \nprogresses. If the MCS can hold together through the morning\nhours and moves into this more primed area, currently over the\nSouthern Tier into NEPA, we could see strong to damaging winds\nwith isolated tornadoes during the afternoon and evening hours.\nAt this same time, a low pressure system will track to our north\nacross Canada, allowing scattered showers and storms to develop\nnorth of the Southern Tier. With severe parameters here lacking,\nespecially the shear, severe storm development chances are much\nlower. Thats not to say an isolated storm may develop a\nmicroburst that could produce damaging winds. To go along with\nthe severe threat, recent rains have left the area saturated.\nPWATs are expected to be about 1.5in through much of the day, so\nheavy rain leading to isolated flash flooding will be possible. \n\nTemperatures will be in the upper 70s to low 80s north of the\nSouthern Tier with low to mid 80s across the Southern Tier and\nNEPA. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure is currently progged to fill in behind the MCS and\nlow pressure system moving through the area on Monday. This\nshould lead to quite a lovely day as much of the active weather\nwill be to our south as another MCS is forecast to roll through\nOH, PA and into the Virginias. Tuesday should see partly sunny \nskies and highs in the upper 70s to low 80s and dewpoints in the\nupper 50s to low 60s.\n\nActive weather returns again mid-week as a trough digs into the\narea, bringing scattered showers and storms. Guidance is \nuncertain on the timing and placement of the trough with some \nhaving it dig south, pushing cool air into the region to end the\nweek, while others have the ridge to the west winning out and \nkeeping the trough farther north with warmer temperatures.\nShower and storm coverage and strength will depend on the \ntiming and location of the trough, so confidence in this \nremains low given the low confidence in guidance's current \nhandling of the trough. Most of the guidance does point to a \nridge building over the area by Saturday, allowing cooler air to\nfilter in by the weekend, with highs in the low to mid 70s for \nboth Saturday and Sunday.",
